course: reflection
lesson: 3
title: Living with loss: keeping memories, and loss that isn't death
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After a loss, the things you used to do with someone can hurt at first, and later turn into the very things that make you feel close to them.
explain: Ryan's grandad always took him fishing; the first time Ryan went alone he sat by the river thinking, "It's not the same. I don't even like it without him," and cried into his flask of tea.
explain: Months later he started talking out loud to Grandad at the river, and fishing began to feel like being near him again; keeping a tradition "in his memory" turned the hurt into a link.
explain: Loss is not only death: a best friend moving abroad, a dog that has to be rehomed, a mum changed by illness, or a family that goes silent can all leave a hole, and the feelings are real even when nobody has died.
explain: Sad feelings also come back around anniversaries, which is not "going backwards"; and when people say clumsy things like "I know how you feel", they are usually trying to connect.
fact: Doing things you did with someone who died can hurt at first, then become a way to feel close to them; this is called keeping a tradition.
fact: Illness can change how a person looks; chemo, a medicine for cancer, can make hair fall out, but the person inside is the same.
fact: Being scared of how an ill parent looks does not make a child mean; asking questions usually helps more than pretending not to notice.
fact: Grown-ups cry too, especially about something they loved for a long time; seeing a parent cry can feel strange, but it is human.
fact: A friend moving far away is a real loss; making a new friend later does not betray the old one.
fact: Small kind people in our lives, like a neighbour, a shopkeeper or a bus driver, can matter a lot, and it is normal to feel sad when they die.
fact: Some grown-ups find it too painful ("too raw") to talk about someone who died; their silence does not mean the person was bad.
fact: Finding another person who can talk and share stories, like a gran, often helps when the rest of the family goes quiet.
fact: To be rehomed means to be given to another family to look after; an allergy is when your body reacts badly to something, such as animal fur.
fact: Being angry at a brother or sister for something they cannot help, like an allergy, is normal, but it is not fair to them.
fact: Comforting phrases like "she's watching over you" can sometimes make a child worry, and it is fine to ask what grown-ups mean.
fact: Sad feelings often return around the anniversary of a death; it does not mean you are going backwards.
fact: People sometimes say "I know how you feel" to show they care, even when their loss was different; every loss is different.
exercise: Ryan goes fishing alone after Grandad dies and hates it. What happened after a few months? | Fishing started to feel like being near Grandad again | He gave up fishing forever and forgot his grandad | He decided fishing was boring without Grandad and sold all of his rods | He started fishing only to win prizes instead
exercise: What did NOT help Ryan with his loss? | Stopping everything he and Grandad used to do | Going back to the same spot by the river where they always fished | Talking out loud to Grandad by the water as if he were still there | Remembering their fishing trips and keeping them "in his memory"
exercise: Megan's mum has cancer and lost her hair from chemo. Megan is scared of how she looks. Is Megan mean? | No; being scared of a change is not mean, and Mum is still Mum | Yes; good children are never scared of anything about their own parents | Yes; she should hide her fear forever so Mum never knows | No, because chemo makes the person inside change as well
exercise: What helped Megan most? | Asking questions and helping Mum choose funny hats | Pretending not to notice that Mum's hair had gone | Staying at a friend's house until the hair grew back | Never looking at her mum's head again at all
exercise: Tyler's tortoise dies after 40 years and Dad cries; Tyler has never seen him cry before. What is true? | Grown-ups cry too, especially about something loved for decades | Dads who cry must be very ill and need a doctor at once | Crying shows Dad is weak and Tyler should look away | Grown men never cry, so Dad is only pretending
exercise: Zara's best friend moved to another country. Months later Zara makes a new friend and feels guilty. What is true? | A new friend does not betray the old one | She has broken a promise and should drop the new friend | Long-distance friends always end up hating each other | She is only allowed one best friend in her whole life
exercise: Ollie's neighbour Mrs Patel, who gave him sweets, has died. He didn't wave the last time. What is true? | Missing a kind neighbour is normal; one rushed day is small | Neighbours don't really count, so he shouldn't feel anything | He should feel bad about that one rushed day forever | He must never be in a hurry again in case someone dies
exercise: What helped Ollie with his small, sad feeling? | Giving a card to Mrs Patel's family and waving more at neighbours | Walking a different way to school so he never has to see her front door again | Blaming himself every time he passes her door | Telling himself she probably never liked him anyway
exercise: Sophie's mum changes the subject whenever Sophie says Grandpa's name. Why might that be? | It is still too painful for Mum to talk about him | Grandpa must have been a bad man that nobody liked | Mum has already forgotten who Grandpa was | Sophie is not allowed to know she had a grandpa
exercise: What helped Sophie when her mum could not talk? | Asking Gran, who told stories, and making a memory jar | Staying silent about Grandpa until everyone in the family forgot him | Asking Mum the same question again and again until she finally gave in | Deciding that Grandpa must have done something bad that nobody mentions
exercise: Harry's dog Buster is rehomed because his little sister has a bad allergy. What does "rehomed" mean? | Given to another family to look after | Taken to the vet so that he could be put to sleep | Sent away to a special dog school for a whole year | Moved into a kennel in the back garden
exercise: Harry is angry at his sister because Buster had to go. What is true? | It's normal to feel angry, but it isn't fair, since she can't help her allergy | She chose to be allergic on purpose so she could get rid of the dog | His anger proves he loves the dog more than his own sister | He should stay angry until the dog comes back home
exercise: Maya is told that Gran is "watching over" her and starts worrying, "So she sees all the bad stuff?" What helped? | Asking Mum, who said Gran loved her even when she was naughty | Being extra perfect so Gran never sees anything bad again | Worrying about it alone every night in bed | Never thinking about Gran at all in case she is watching
exercise: A year after his dad died, Liam suddenly feels very sad again around the same date. Why? | Sad feelings often return around anniversaries | He has gone back to the start and must grieve all over again | He was faking being fine for the whole year | It means something new and terrible is about to happen
exercise: What helped Liam on the anniversary? | Doing his dad's favourite walk and eating his favourite chips | Being told firmly by a relative that he should really be over it by now | Pretending it was a normal day and ignoring it | Staying in bed all day so the date would pass
exercise: Ruby's friend says, "My nan died too, it's the same." Ruby is annoyed. What is the kindest true answer? | "Thanks, it feels different for me," because her friend was trying to help | "Shut up, you never even saw your nan, so you can't talk" | "You're right, every loss is exactly the same" | Nothing; she should stop being friends with her
test: Ryan's mum says, "It's what Grandad would want, love, you going down the river." What does she mean? | Keeping their fishing tradition can be a way to remember him | Grandad left a rule in his will that Ryan must go fishing every day | Ryan will be punished by the family if he ever stops going fishing | Grandad will somehow come back to the river if Ryan keeps on fishing
test: Megan's cousin goes, "ngl your mum looks sus without hair, kinda freaky." ("Ngl" means not gonna lie; "sus" means suspicious, odd.) What's true? | Chemo can make hair fall out, but the person inside is the same | Losing hair means she has turned into a different person | People who lose their hair are always about to die | Megan's mum chose to shave her head to frighten people
test: Tyler's mate says, "my dad would NEVER cry, real men don't." Is that right? | No; grown men cry too, about things they loved long | Yes; crying is only for women and small children | Yes; men stop being able to cry when they turn thirty | No, but men only cry when they are physically hurt
test: In a cartoon, a girl's best friend moves away and the girl swears never to have another friend. Is this a good idea? | No; making a new friend doesn't betray the old one | Yes; having a new friend would be a kind of cheating | Yes; friends who move away always come back soon | No, because she should forget the old friend at once
test: What would happen if Harry kept blaming his sister for Buster leaving? | He would be unfair to her, and it would hurt them both | His sister would grow out of her allergy faster if he kept on at her | Buster would be sent back to live with the family again | Nothing bad; blaming somebody always helps sad feelings get better
test: Liam's teacher in Newcastle says, "Howay, pet, it's been a year, you should be canny now." ("Howay" means come on; "canny" here means fine.) What's the truth? | Grief doesn't follow a timetable; feelings often return near anniversaries | The teacher is right; grief must end after exactly one year | Liam is going backwards and needs to start again | Feelings only come back if a person wants them to
test: Sophie's family never says Grandpa's name. What would happen if Sophie also stayed silent forever? | She might stay confused and lonely with her questions | She would learn the full truth about Grandpa faster by keeping quiet | Her mum would feel better by never hearing his name | Grandpa's memory would be kept safest that way
test: Zara texts at midnight: "she's 8 hours ahead now, it's like she's dead." What could help? | Calls at planned times, letters and a shared online game | Deleting her friend's number so it hurts less | Never talking to anyone about the move ever | Staying awake all night every night until the two time zones match up
test: Maya's gran's friend, a proper West Country lady, says, "Your gran's watchin' over 'ee, my lover." ("My lover" is just a friendly way to say "dear".) Maya worries. What should she do? | Ask what it means; it's meant as comfort, not as a camera on her | Be perfect forever, so that her gran never sees her do anything bad at all | Decide the lady is being rude and refuse to talk to her | Hide in her room so that nobody can watch her any more
test: Ruby snaps "it's NOT the same!" at her friend, who goes quiet. What does Ruby understand weeks later? | Her friend was trying to connect and show she cared | Her friend was trying to make fun of her grief | Her friend's nan never really died at all | Her friend wanted to win a competition about loss
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of continuing activities formerly shared with a deceased person? | Such activities may initially cause pain and subsequently afford a sense of closeness | Such activities invariably prolong the period of grief without limit | Such activities are prohibited until a full year has elapsed | Such activities have no bearing whatsoever upon the bereaved
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an adult may decline to discuss a deceased relative. | The subject remains too painful for that adult to address | The deceased has necessarily been found guilty of misconduct | Adults are forbidden by law to mention deceased relatives | The adult has entirely forgotten the existence of the deceased
exam: It is given that a child experiences renewed sorrow on the anniversary of a parent's death. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Such recurrence is common and does not constitute regression | Such recurrence demonstrates that the grief was previously feigned | Such recurrence indicates that the grief shall never diminish | Such recurrence is experienced only by persons who are unwell
exam: Ascertain which of the following is consistent with the facts concerning a friendship formed after a former friend has emigrated. | The new friendship does not constitute a betrayal of the former one | The new friendship must necessarily be terminated upon the former friend's return | The new friendship is invalid pending the former friend's consent | The new friendship necessarily signifies that the former has ended
